Interior eigenvalue problems arise in many scientific and engineering applications. This paper presents AdaPolySI, an adaptive polynomial-filtered subspace iteration eigensolver for large Hermitian interior eigenvalue problems, based on a Chebyshev approximation of the step function. AdaPolySI dynamically adjusts the polynomial degree as the iteration progresses, thereby reducing the number of sparse matrix–vector multiplications (SpMVs). We provide a theoretical convergence analysis and a practical strategy for selecting the polynomial degree. The performance is further improved through optimized sparse–dense matrix multiplication (SpMM) and efficient deflation techniques. Extensive experiments on Intel and ARM HPC systems demonstrate that AdaPolySI outperforms state-of-the-art solvers, including our implementation of the CJ-FEAST method as well as polynomial subspace method in EVSL and SLEPc. Both strong and weak scalability results are presented to demonstrate the parallel efficiency of AdaPolySI.